    "So far you have mostly talked about what you **do** (the present) and what you are **going to do** (the near future). The **preterite** (*el pretérito*) is the tense for telling stories: what you did yesterday, last weekend, on holiday. It describes **finished actions at a definite time in the past** — 'I went', 'we played', 'it was great'.\n\nIn this topic you'll learn the regular endings for *-ar*, *-er* and *-ir* verbs, the spelling changes in *jugué* and *toqué*, the big irregulars (*fui, hice, tuve, estuve*), the time phrases that signal the past, and finally how to move between past, present and future in one piece of writing.\n\nBy the end you should be able to write a paragraph like this, and understand every word:\n\n    El sábado pasado fui al centro con mis amigos. Primero jugamos al baloncesto\n    y luego comí churros con chocolate. Por la tarde llovió mucho, pero lo pasé bomba.\n\n> **Accents matter more here than anywhere else.** *Hablo* means 'I speak'; *habló* means 'he spoke'. One small mark changes the person **and** the time.",
